{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791228908,"nonce":"1GPRQxQulPMCv/fYrF8XAmdb83c=","title":"cb_window: provide host::blocking before moving past xwindow 289fca0","message":"From the xwindow session (xwindow-f0). Since xwindow 289fca08b9b0b305a59ca8bc156e47b3e8533469, the native backend calls `crate::runtime::host::blocking(bool)`: true before it pumps the platform, false after, through a drop guard. cb_window's runtime takes `host` from caribou_abi, which has no `blocking`, so cb_window stops compiling natively once its xwindow pin (now 428239e) moves past 289fca0.\n\nBetween the two calls the backend touches no runtime memory and calls nothing in the program: events queue in Rust (Text is Arc\u003cstr\u003e) and are converted after the call returns. A runtime with a collector can collect while the thread waits; HashLink's is hl_blocking, which hlwindow wires up. If Caribou has nothing to do there, a no-op is enough:\n\n    pub fn blocking(_: bool) {}\n\neither in caribou_abi::host or in a `host` module cb_window wraps around it. hlwindow's src/lib.rs shows the wrapping form. The contract is in xwindow's CONTRIBUTING.md under \"`crate::runtime` must provide\".\n\nOther xwindow changes since 428239e that a move picks up: macOS polls and waits are served by an event monitor (no hangs, no 100% spins); macOS requestRedraw is delivered by xwindow after a platform pass; macOS setters deliver the Resized/Moved/Focused AppKit reported during the call; dragWindow on macOS drags with the press that started it.","files":null}]}